::CW_U02_L07_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Passage: Maya stood outside the closed gym doors with the debate trophy under one arm. Inside, her teammates were laughing. She had won the final round, but she had also ignored Kai's signal to let him answer the last question. Which choice best shows an integrated scene plan?{
~A. Purpose: show Maya is happy; Point of view: any; Key element: trophy; Pacing: fast only.#Incorrect. This names pieces, but they do not explain the conflict or how the reader should understand Maya's choice.
~B. Purpose: describe the gym; Point of view: narrator far away; Key element: doors; Pacing: many descriptions.#Incorrect. The gym may matter, but the central pressure is Maya's win and her damaged teamwork.
~C. Purpose: explain debate rules; Point of view: second person; Key element: final question; Pacing: summary.#Incorrect. Debate rules are background. The scene needs choices that reveal character, conflict, and meaning.
=D. Purpose: show Maya realizing success can still cost trust; Point of view: close third on Maya; Key element: trophy pressed against her side; Pacing: slow at the doors, then faster when she enters.#Correct. This connects purpose, narrator focus, key element, and pacing so each craft choice supports the same meaning.
}

::CW_U02_L07_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Passage: The narrator says, "I kept the cracked compass in my pocket because Dad said it always found the truth. When it spun wildly beside the old train station, I almost turned back." What is the strongest scene purpose?{
~A. To list all the places the character has traveled.#Incorrect. The passage focuses on one meaningful moment, not a travel list.
~B. To prove the compass is scientifically accurate.#Incorrect. The compass works symbolically in the scene; the important issue is the character's decision and fear.
=C. To show a character facing uncertainty through a meaningful object.#Correct. The compass, the memory of Dad, and the choice to continue all work together to create purpose.
~D. To describe the train station architecture in detail.#Incorrect. The station is setting, but the passage does not make architecture the main purpose.
}

::CW_U02_L07_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Passage: "Nora rehearsed the apology three times before school. When Eli walked by her locker, she opened her mouth, but he handed her the missing sketchbook first." Which event is the turning point?{
=A. Eli hands Nora the missing sketchbook.#Correct. This changes what Nora expected and forces her to respond in a new way.
~B. Nora rehearses before school.#Incorrect. This prepares the conflict, but it does not change the direction of the scene.
~C. Nora stands by her locker.#Incorrect. This gives the scene a place, but it is not the shift in action or understanding.
~D. Nora opens her mouth.#Incorrect. This begins her intended action, but the real turn happens when Eli interrupts with the sketchbook.
}

::CW_U02_L07_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Passage: Scene goal: show that a character feels trapped by a family expectation. Draft sentence: "The dining room clock clicked louder each time Uncle Ren asked whether I had applied to medical school." Which revision best strengthens alignment?{
~A. The dining room had four chairs, a window, and a rug.#Incorrect. This adds neutral details but does not increase the feeling of pressure.
=B. The clock clicked like a lock each time Uncle Ren repeated, "medical school."#Correct. The revised image connects setting, sound, dialogue, and the trapped feeling.
~C. Uncle Ren liked dinner, and the narrator liked school.#Incorrect. This tells background information without sharpening the scene's conflict.
~D. I thought about medical school and many other careers.#Incorrect. This is broad summary. The scene needs a focused detail that supports feeling trapped.
}

::CW_U02_L07_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Passage: A student writes a scene about a runner deciding whether to quit a race. The scene includes a close first-person narrator, the repeated image of loosened shoelaces, and a slow paragraph before the runner chooses to continue. What is the best feedback?{
~A. Remove the shoelaces because objects should not carry meaning.#Incorrect. A repeated object can help reveal pressure or motivation when it connects to the scene's purpose.
~B. Change to an all-knowing narrator because first person is never useful in tense scenes.#Incorrect. First person can be very effective when the goal is to show immediate inner conflict.
~C. Add a long history of every race the runner has entered.#Incorrect. Background can help, but too much history may slow the turning point and weaken focus.
=D. Check that the shoelaces, inner thoughts, and pacing all point to the same decision pressure.#Correct. Integrated scenes work when each craft choice supports the central moment instead of pulling attention away.
}
